CORE
main.c
--unwind 4 --rounds 4 --allow-pointer-unsoundness --refined-pointer-analysis
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
A pointer published by a thread symexed later was invisible to an earlier
read -- CBMC issue #305.

Value sets are built in symex order. `reader` is symexed first, so its
`p = gp` records the only target `gp` has at that moment, `&n1`. `updater`
publishes `&n2` afterwards, which repairs `gp`'s own entry but not `p`'s:
nothing revisits a local that was assigned from the pointer before the
publication existed. `p->x` then resolves against `n1` alone and the
interleaving where the reader runs last is never encoded, so the assertion
appears to hold.

Only a previous pass can know what gets published, which is what
--refined-pointer-analysis collects: it iterates symex until the union of
every state's value set stops growing. The bug was in how it applied the
result -- it unioned that whole set into every state at every step, widening
every pointer everywhere. Measured, that tripled the formula and suppressed
race detection in both directions: it removed `libvsync/hemlock`'s real race
exactly as readily as `mcslock`'s spurious one, and invented false alarms of
its own.

It is now applied where the staleness actually bites: at a shared read of a
pointer, that one object's entry is topped up from the collected set, and
nothing else is touched. On this program the race is found either way; what
the narrower rule buys is that `hemlock` and
`28-race_reach_82-list_racefree` keep their correct answers, which the wide
version took away.

The flag remains off by default. Without it there is no previous pass, so
there is nothing to seed from and the behaviour is unchanged.
